while the solution \((u_{h},p_{h})\) of \((\mathsf{Q}_{h})\) is characterized by:

\[\mathscr{L}(u_{h},p_{h})=\mathop{\mathrm{Min}}\limits_{v_{h}\in X_{h}}\sup_{q_{ h}\in M_{h}}\mathscr{L}(v_{h},q_{h})=\mathop{\mathrm{Max}}\limits_{q_{h}\in M_{h}} \inf_{v_{h}\in X_{h}}\mathscr{L}(v_{h},q_{h}).\]

_Remark 1.3_.: From the argument of Theorem 1.1, we readily derive that if Hypotheses (1.10) and (1.12) hold then the solution \((u_{h},\lambda_{h})\) is bounded as follows:

\[\|u_{h}\|_{X}\leqslant \frac{1}{\alpha^{*}}\biggl{\{}\|\,l\|_{X^{\prime}}+\frac{1}{\beta^{ *}}(\alpha^{*}+\|\,a\|)\,\|\,\chi\|_{M^{\prime}}\biggr{\}},\] \[\|\,\lambda_{h}\|_{M}\leqslant \frac{1}{\beta^{*}}\,\{\|\,l\|_{X^{\prime}}+\|\,a\|\,\|\,u_{h}\|_{X }\}.\]

Observe that the bilinear form \(a(.,.)\) is \(V_{h}\)-elliptic as soon as \(a(v_{h},v_{h})>0\) for all \(v_{h}\neq 0\). Similarly, the bilinear form \(b(.,.)\) satisfies the discrete inf-sup condition (1.12) provided \(\operatorname{Ker}(B^{\prime}_{h})\cap M_{h}=\{0\}\). But of course in either case the constants \(\alpha^{*}\) and \(\beta^{*}\) will generally depend upon \(h\). Now, in order to derive optimal error bounds in Theorem 1.1, it is clear that both constants \(\alpha^{*}\) and \(\beta^{*}\) must be independent of \(h\). And since usually \(V_{h}\notin V\), the \(V\)-ellipticity of \(a(.,.)\) does not necessarily carry over to \(V_{h}\). As a consequence, hypothesis (1.10) must be checked in each particular case; but for the applications we have in mind, this is not a major obstacle. On the other hand, the discrete inf-sup condition (1.12) which acts as a uniform compatibility condition between \(X_{h}\) and \(M_{h}\) is much more delicate to check. The following lemma due to Fortin [28] establishes a useful criterion for (1.12).

**Lemma 1.1**.: _The inf-sup condition (1.12) holds with a constant \(\beta^{*}>0\) independent of \(h\) if and only if there exists an operator \(\Pi_{h}\in\mathscr{L}(X;X_{h})\) satisfying:_

(1.19) \[b(v-\Pi_{h}v,\mu_{h})=0\quad\quad\forall\mu_{h}\in M_{h},\quad\quad\forall v \in X\]

_and_

(1.20) \[\|\,\Pi_{h}v\,\|_{X}\leqslant C\,\|\,v\,\|_{X}\quad\quad\forall v\in X\]

_with a constant \(C>0\) independent of \(h\)._

Proof.: Assume that such an operator \(\Pi_{h}\) exists; then we have for all \(\mu_{h}\in M_{h}\):

\[\sup_{v_{h}\in X_{h}}\frac{b(v_{h},\mu_{h})}{\|\,v_{h}\|_{X}}\geqslant\sup_{v \in X}\frac{b(\Pi_{h}v,\mu_{h})}{\|\,\Pi_{h}v\,\|_{X}}=\sup_{v\in X}\frac{b(v, \mu_{h})}{\|\,\Pi_{h}v\,\|_{X}},\]

owing to (1.19). Thus (1.20) and (1.5) imply that

\[\sup_{v_{h}\in X_{h}}\frac{b(v_{h},\mu_{h})}{\|\,v_{h}\|_{X}}\geqslant\frac{1} {C}\sup_{v\in X}\frac{b(v,\mu_{h})}{\|\,v\,\|_{X}}\geqslant\frac{\beta}{C}\,\| \,\mu_{h}\|_{M},\]

and (1.12) follows with \(\beta^{*}=\beta/C\).
